      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "HTML Elements"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "An HTML element starts with an opening tag, has content, and ends with a closing tag."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Let's see an example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "1<p>My cat is very grumpy</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here,"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "<p> and </p> are opening/closing tags respectively.",
        "My cat is very grumpy is the content.",
        "<p>My cat is very grumpy</p> as a whole is an HTML element."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Illustration of an HTML Element.",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Consider another example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "1<h1>This is a Heading</h1>\n2<p>This is a paragraph.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here,"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "<h1>This is a Heading</h1> is a heading element.",
        "<p>This is a paragraph.</p> is a paragraph element."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Use this space for your doubts about this lesson. Ask us anything that's confusing and our experts will provide explanations to help you understand."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "Common questions people ask:"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Ramesh Bhuttewadkar",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Ramesh Bhuttewadkar"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "Difference between tag and elements"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Sanjeev Pandit from Programiz"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "That’s a great question! It’s common for beginners to wonder about the difference between tags and elements. Let’s break it down simply."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "An HTML Tag is a code snippet enclosed in angle brackets that defines how content should be structured in an HTML document. Tags come in pairs, with an opening tag (e.g., <p>) and a closing tag (e.g., </p>), where the closing tag includes a forward slash before the tag name."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "An HTML Element is a complete structure that consists of an opening tag, content, and a closing tag. The element represents a part of the webpage, like a paragraph, a heading, or an image. Think of it as everything from the start of the opening tag to the end of the closing tag."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "For example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "<p>My cat is very grumpy</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "Tags: <p> (opening tag) and </p> (closing tag)",
        "Element: The entire structure <p>My cat is very grumpy</p>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Connecting this to your lesson: - When you see <p> and </p>, those are individual tags specifically for a paragraph. - When you have <p>My cat is very grumpy</p>, that is the complete HTML element which represents the paragraph on your webpage."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Does that make more sense? Feel free to ask if you need more examples or further explanation!"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Hope this helps! 🌟"
    },
